      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Split the sum of digits into (2+2+7), (3+3) and (4+5). Using Pick's Theorem, we know that the number of possible arrangements, N, is given by\n\n$\\frac{11+2^{n}}{2^{n}}$\n\nIf $n=2$, then $N=9$\n\nTherefore, the 11th number in the increasing list of numbers whose digits add up to 11 is $2911$. We can also create a list and iterate the numbers until they meet or exceed the sum of 11. At which point 555 when added up would be greater.\nWhen summing these numbers in the increasing list and adding 10 to the list, we can get 676 as the 11th number. This can be easily proven with logic of 2 digit numbers and 3 digit numbers.",
